  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Gage Jump gave them six innings of three-hit baseball, and they didn't waste it.",
    "what_this_means": "The margin was always there. They led from the second inning on, never trailed, and closed it out with three straight scoreless frames from Harris, Medina, and Alvarado. Carlos Cortes drove in both runs that mattered with a home run. Lawrence Butler went 2-for-4. The bullpen held. This is what a clean game looks like for a team that hasn't had many of them. Jump's line. Nine strikeouts, two earned runs, six innings. Was the kind of start that wins games when the offense doesn't fold. They didn't fold. Cortes converted two of their four RISP opportunities into runs, and that was enough. The fragility in the trend is real, but so is the ability to execute when the pieces align. Four-run lead they never gave back. That's a win, not a survival.",
    "what_to_watch": "Minnesota starts Connor Prielipp tonight at 6:05 PT. A 5.57 ERA over a season that has not been kind to either team's pitching. This is a chance to build on clean baseball, not abandon it.",
